From 756b962ee1f1f7a833f0db6408cdce8477f4dfef Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Michael Stufflebeam <cpuchip@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 17:17:48 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/5] 055 (1/5): WAMR pinned, with its patch series
 (third_party/wamr, scripts/fetch-wamr.sh)

Decision 67 (Michael, abacus a6a3c73, #5322): "WAMR, and send the fix upstream too". third_party/wamr/PIN
names WAMR main ea5dd88 and five patches by sha256, and the tree they must make (13f26968).
scripts/fetch-wamr.sh exports that commit from a local clone (WAMR_REPO, default ../wasm-micro-runtime) in a
private object store, applies the patches in order and writes build/wamr only when the result is PIN's tree;
a patch with another hash, a commit not on the clone's origin or another tree writes nothing. The clone is
never written to. The patches are WAMR's code, under its licence (Apache-2.0 WITH LLVM-exception):
- 0001, PSRAM AOT code mapped on the instruction bus (WAMR PR #5146, issue #5145);
- 0002, the Xtensa L32R relocation lost to strict aliasing (PR #5148, issue #5147);
- 0003, os_self_thread() returns the FreeRTOS task handle, so apps on the calc task can call AOT code (local;
  soroban #5413, accepted abacus #5450);
- 0004, the target symbol maps const, so they sit in flash (local; accepted abacus #5508);
- 0005, a project without libc-wasi may leave lwip out (local; accepted abacus #5541).
The audits of 0003-0005 are in stu32-arcade probes/wasm/. Nothing builds this until STU_WASM (2/5).

Written by arcade for soroban's gate.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
